==============================================================================
[1] how big is the ignition feasible set, and does it contain the truth?
==============================================================================

  Coverage is 100 % by construction for the three centred regimes: each
  interval is built around the rate the true ignition implies, so the truth
  is inside it by definition. The informative column is the area. Only the
  history regime, whose interval comes from the fire's own growth and not
  from the answer, has a coverage number that means anything.

           rate known to   median area   as % of burn   covers truth
        oracle (+/-10 %)       14.3 km2           34 %           100 %
      covariates (x1.96)       47.1 km2          100 %           100 %
      prior only (x2.01)       47.1 km2          100 %           100 %
         history (x3.21)       38.1 km2           94 %            85 %

  Knowing the rate to +/-10 % instead of the factor of 1.96 an investigator
  actually has shrinks the feasible ignition area by 3x, from 47 to 14.3 km2.
  And +/-10 % on the rate is exactly what section 1 says a burn scar cannot
  give you, because the rate it hands over is a product of fuel and wind.

  The covariates are worth almost nothing: 47 km2 against 47 km2 for knowing nothing
  about the fire at all -- which is section 14.3's R^2 = 0.084, in units an
  investigator can act on.

==============================================================================
[2] does it shrink as the fire is watched for longer? (plan item P5)
==============================================================================

    The absolute area is reported beside the fraction of the burn it covers,
    because a fire that has run longer is bigger and its feasible set would
    grow on that account alone. The fraction is what controls for it.

    days burning  backtracks       burn                oracle           covariates           prior only
           3 - 5         333      25km2          7.2 ( 32%)        25.0 (100%)        25.0 (100%)
           6 - 9         447      30km2          8.9 ( 33%)        29.7 (100%)        29.7 (100%)
         10 - 15         508      40km2         12.2 ( 35%)        39.8 (100%)        39.8 (100%)
           >= 16         847     106km2         40.4 ( 36%)       106.3 (100%)       106.3 (100%)

  The feasible set is a *region*, never a point. Its absolute area grows 5.6x
  from the shortest to the longest window, but so does the burn (4.2x), and as a
  fraction of the burn it goes 32 % -> 36 % under the oracle and stays at 100 % under the
  covariates. So watching the fire for longer does not localise where it
  started; the plan's P5 predicted a region rather than a point, and
  predicted the wrong direction for how that region behaves.

  The operational reading: backtrack from the *earliest* perimeter you
  have, not the latest. The smallest absolute feasible set here comes from
  the 3-5 day window, at 7.2 km2 under an oracle rate and 25.0 km2 with the
  rate an investigator can actually obtain.

